    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["R3"],
      "pins": ["U2.1", "U3.1", "J4.4", "J1.3"],
      "nets": ["RST", "VCC", "+5V"],
      "observed": "R3 (10K) is drawn as a pull-up from the RST net to the VCC net. VCC is the LM1117-5.0 input / fan-supply rail (U1: VI = VCC, VO = +5V), while both microcontrollers are powered from +5V (U2 pin 8 = +5V, U3 pin 8 = +5V).",
      "expected": "A reset pull-up must return to the microcontroller's own supply rail, i.e. +5V (the LM1117-5.0 output), not to the higher-voltage fan rail VCC. The reset line should sit at the 5 V logic level, not above it.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Sheet 1: R3 10K pull-up symbol labelled VCC, bottom node labelled RST; C1/C2 100nF also returned to VCC"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Sheet 1: U1 LM1117-5.0, VI (pin 3) = VCC, VO (pin 2) = +5V; U2 ATtiny45-20P pin 8 VCC = +5V; U3 ATtiny13V-10S pin 8 VCC = +5V"}
      ],
      "impact": "The ATtiny RESET/PB5 pin (and the ISP RST pin on J4) is pulled toward the higher VCC rail through R3 instead of the 5 V rail, so the pin is driven above its own VCC and is clamped only by the MCU's internal ESD diode, producing continuous clamp current and a potential over-voltage/reliability failure; the reset line no longer rests at a clean 5 V logic level.",
      "confidence": 0.4
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design is largely coherent (ATtiny45/ATtiny13 option, ISP header wired correctly, LM1117-5.0 making +5V from the VCC fan rail), but the reset pull-up R3 returns to the VCC fan rail rather than the +5V MCU rail, pulling the RESET pin above its own supply; this is the most likely deviation found in the drawing."
}

